WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.430
SPEAKER: --a particularly
persuasive story,

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.700
and to onboard stakeholders
or other partners,

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.840
or actually to make an
effective pitch, why

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.880
we need to focus on this
particular area versus another.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.570
So again, implementing
these data-driven decisions

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:18.420
with an eye towards
other skill sets again,

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.032
like along the lines
of storytelling.

00:00:20.032 --> 00:00:21.990
JENNY OLDFIELD: I think
that's absolutely true.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:25.530
One of the problems of this
area is that data is complex.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.450
Technology is complex.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.730
It confuses people,
whereas we need

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.206
to give vaccinations to a
child is easy to understand.

00:00:32.206 --> 00:00:34.080
We need to feed people
is easy to understand.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.350
So there's a challenge to us to
communicate why this matters.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:40.390
But in terms of people
interested to get involved,

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:41.670
there are so many ways.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.310
And there are a number of
different organizations

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.860
that allow you to donate
your time without needing

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.430
to be physically in the space,
to do things like playing games

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:53.880
that help identify
malaria through looking

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:58.209
at different sequences to
mapping different areas.

00:00:58.209 --> 00:00:59.750
One of the big
problems is that there

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:02.610
are whole parts of the world
that aren't mapped and could

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:05.220
do with being better captured.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.710
So if you only have
a few hours a week,

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.450
and you're interested
in this, then there

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.125
are many different
ways to get involved.

00:01:11.125 --> 00:01:11.708
SPEAKER: Yeah.

00:01:11.708 --> 00:01:15.990
And to add opportunities,
beyond this area of expertise

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.270
question, there's
also opportunities

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.797
to participate in the Data for
Good movement through Kaggle

00:01:21.797 --> 00:01:23.630
and also just learn
more about data science.

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.770
So Kaggle, for those
unfamiliar with the platform,

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.660
is a community
platform to actually

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.560
problem-solve together, to
use their workbench solution

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.740
of kernels to analyze data sets
and actually show your work,

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.262
so to speak, as you go
through that analysis,

00:01:38.262 --> 00:01:40.470
and also be able to engage
with other members who are

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:42.420
participating on the platform.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.960
Kaggle also launched a series
of Data Science for Good events.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.610
So if you are a data scientist
or burgeoning data scientist,

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.940
you can explore those
challenges and put your skills

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.550
to work there too.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:58.380
Another organization that we
have worked with in the past,

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.860
and actually presented a webinar
with previously back in--

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.210
I think it was
about June or July--

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:04.920
is DataKind.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.310
So DataKind-- they do
a lot of great work.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:09.870
They have a large--

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.960
I think last time I checked
over 18,000 volunteers

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:14.470
as part of their network.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:16.950
And they actually match pro
bono data scientists, analysts,

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.740
designers, et cetera, with
mission-driven organizations

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:24.010
that are facing a particular
data analytics challenge.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.980
So that's another great
way to get involved.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:26.790
JENNY OLDFIELD: Absolutely.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:28.360
SPEAKER: And the
partnership, of course.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:29.193
JENNY OLDFIELD: Yes.

00:02:29.193 --> 00:02:31.960
The partnership is
focused on organization.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.527
So I would encourage anyone
working institutionally

00:02:34.527 --> 00:02:35.610
who wants to get involved.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.710
But as I mentioned,
many of our partners--

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:42.240
so if you come to
www.data4sdgs.org,

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.970
then you can see all of our
partners, and a number of them

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:48.080
focus on individuals and
how they can get involved.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.160
SPEAKER: Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:50.660
Wonderful.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:53.419
Well, thank you again
for your time today.

00:02:53.419 --> 00:02:54.960
It was great to have
this opportunity

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.719
to have Jenny be present and
share the work that she's

00:02:57.719 --> 00:02:59.760
doing with the Global
Partnership for Sustainable

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.887
Development Data and also
touch on the initiatives

00:03:01.887 --> 00:03:02.970
that we launched recently.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.720
Again, those are Data
Solutions for Change

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.740
for Nonprofits and Visualize
2030, a data storytelling

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.050
contest for students
at the higher ed level.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:13.950
So with that, stay tuned
for the next session,

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:17.660
and continue to keep up
with Cloud OnAir webinars.

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:18.160
Thank you.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.100
JENNY OLDFIELD: Thank you.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.850
